1:# Documentation — data provenance, inputs, and results
2:
3:Companion to `README.md`, which covers folder layout. This file covers where the
4:data came from, what each table needs, how each table is computed, and what the
5:numbers mean.
6:
7:---
8:
9:## 1. The research question
10:
11:All three linkages test the same thing: **does a better transcription of census
12:name fields produce better record linkage?** Each linkage was run twice — once on
13:the *old* transcription, once on the *new* — and each table compares the two.
14:
15:"Old" and "new" appear under different names depending on the linkage:
16:
17:| Linkage | Old transcription | New transcription |
18:|---|---|---|
19:| ABE / Jaro-Winkler | `..._ans_cleaned` (Ancestry) | `..._true_cleaned` |
20:| HLINK | `matches_before.dta` | `matches_after.dta` |
21:| Mortality | `..._oldnames` | `..._newnames` |
22:
23:---
24:
25:## 2. Data provenance
26:
27:### `data/census/`
28:
29:| File | Size | Origin |
30:|---|---|---|
31:| `1930_1940_full_w_women.dta` | 16.0 GB | IPUMS full-count 1930 + 1940. 254,679,910 persons. Converted from the IPUMS extract `usa_00021.dta.gz` (now in `_archive/data/`). Vars: `year sample serial hhwt gq pernum perwt sex age race raced bpl bpld versionhist histid`. |
32:| `1940_census_for_merge_with_bunmd.dta` | 5.8 GB | Built by `code/03_mortality/000_merge_new_census_vars.do`: `$ABE_SRC/matching_intgen1940.dta` (11.9 GB, now in `abe_jw/data/source_census/`) merged 1:1 on `histid` with `marriage_status_1940.dta`. |
33:| `marriage_status_1940.dta` | 6.2 GB | Built by `code/03_mortality/000_import_new_census_vars.do` from IPUMS extract `usa_00015.dat`. **The raw `.dat` is not on disk** — see Known gaps. |
34:
35:### `data/names/` — `$D_NAMES` and `$D_FLAGS`
36:
37:| File | Size | Origin |
38:|---|---|---|
39:| `cleaned/clean_names_{1930_old,1940_old,1940_new}.dta` | 16.2 GB | The canonical cleaned-name extracts, produced by `code/01_abe_jw/linkage/00_merge_names_to_census.do` (step 0). Men only: 62,079,010 rows (1930) and 66,135,592 (1940). Vars: `age histid mi f_name l_name sex birthyr race bpl`. Originally lived outside the package at `F:\Deaglan\abe_jw\data\clean_names\`; step 0 now writes here directly, so no second copy is made. |
40:| `flags/ipums_fs_names_for_Deaglan1940.dta` | 5.0 GB | `histid` + `congruent`, 122,551,263 rows. `congruent == 0` flags 1940 records where the Ancestry and FamilySearch transcriptions **disagree** — this defines the "restricted" sample in linkages 1 and 2. Separated from the name extracts because it is a flag file, not names. |
41:
42:### `data/transcriptions/` — `$D_TRANSCRIPT` and `$D_APPENDED`
43:
44:The same data at two stages, now stored together:
45:
46:| Path | Size | Origin |
47:|---|---|---|
48:| `shards/` | 40.6 GB | Four folders of 144 `norace<N>.dta` shards each, one per transcription vintage. The four source `.zip` archives are in `_archive/data/transfer_zips/`. |
49:| `appended/divided_file{1930,1940}_{ans,true}_cleaned_women_added.dta` | 42.8 GB | The shards appended, one file per vintage, by `code/prep/append_transfer.do`. 122,771,589 rows (1930) and 131,899,064 (1940) — men **and** women. Carry the census histid in a column named `id`, and the middle initial as `mi` or `mi_true_cleaned`. |
50:
51:These two used to sit in different top-level folders (`transcriptions/` and
52:`Names/`) under different names, which hid the fact that one is simply the
53:appended form of the other.
54:
55:### `data/links/` — `$D_LINKS` and `$D_LINKS_RAW`
56:
57:Split by who produced it: `raw_hlink/` is third-party input, `derived/` is
58:everything this project made.
59:
60:| File | Size | Origin |
61:|---|---|---|
62:| `raw_hlink/step{1,2}_matches_{before,after}.csv` | 33.5 GB | **Raw HLINK output, produced by a third party.** The matching itself was not done here. |
63:| `derived/matches_before.dta` / `matches_after.dta` | 4.7 / 4.8 GB | HLINK links, built by `code/02_hlink/build_matched_links.do` from the four step CSVs. 66,807,539 and 68,498,407 pairs. Vars: `histid1930 histid1940 probability`. |
64:| `derived/JW_matches_x2_unique_{new,old}.dta` | 1.0 GB each | Jaro-Winkler links. 13,478,232 / 12,670,605 pairs. Keys `Ahistid` / `Bhistid`. |
65:| `derived/link1930_1940_{true,ans}_cleaned_noraceblock0.dta` | 2.9 / 2.6 GB | ABE links. 18,198,099 / 16,946,362 pairs. Keys `id_A` / `id_B`, with `fiveyr_band`. Producing code was missing; reconstructed as `code/01_abe_jw/linkage/run_abe_match.do` and verified to reproduce these files exactly on Massachusetts — see Known gaps. |
66:
67:### `data/truth/`
68:
69:| File | Size | Origin |
70:|---|---|---|
71:| `mlp_training1930_1940.dta` | 2.6 GB | Ground-truth 1930→1940 links, used to classify every proposed pair as correct, contradicted, or unverifiable. Every one of the five tables depends on it. |
72:| `mlp_census_crosswalk_v2_0.stata.zip` | 16.0 GB | The **MLP census crosswalk v2.0** — one `.dta` of 62.3 GB uncompressed. Almost certainly the source `mlp_training1930_1940.dta` was extracted from. Kept compressed: nothing reads it directly. |
73:
74:> **The extraction step is not in this package.** No script here derives
75:> `mlp_training1930_1940.dta` from the crosswalk, so the 1930→1940 subsetting
76:> is undocumented. The derived file is present and every table uses it, so
77:> nothing is blocked, but the provenance chain has a gap at this link.
78:
79:### `abe_jw/data/` — the ABE/JW working tree
80:
81:| Path | Size | Contents |
82:|---|---|---|
83:| `by_bpl/`, `by_bpl_new_transcription/` | 28 GB | One folder per birthplace, each with `clean_names_{1930,1940}.dta`. Direct input to both matchers. 136 birthplaces, 128,090,207 records |
84:| `full_census/`, `full_census_new_transcription/` | 38 GB | Prep-stage output plus `names/` — the men-only transcription shards the ABE/JW pipeline reads |
85:| `source_census/` | 21.4 GB | `matching_intgen{1930,1940}.dta`, copied in from `D:\ABE_JW\DATA\ORIGINAL`. Raw input to step 0; **not regenerable from anything in this package** |
86:| `matched/`, `matched_new_transcription/` | 10 GB | JW outputs at both age bands. `JW_matches_x2_unique` is also copied into `data/links/derived/` |
87:| `matched_abe_old/`, `matched_abe_new/` | 0.2 GB | Per-birthplace output from the reconstructed ABE matcher |
88:
89:This tree sits at the project root rather than under `data/` because its scripts
90:`cd "$ABEJW"` and then use relative paths like `"data\matched\..."`. Preserving
91:its internal layout means one global (`ABEJW`) relocates the whole thing.
92:
93:### `data/mortality/`
94:
95:| Path | Size | Contents |
96:|---|---|---|
97:| `raw/bunmd_v2/bunmd_v2.csv` | 7.3 GB | Berkeley Unified Numident Mortality Database v2 |
98:| `raw/censoc_{numident,dmf}_v3.csv` | 1.2 GB | CenSoc public releases |
99:| `intermediate/` | 23 GB | Census with old and new names merged; title/nickname dictionaries |
100:| `working/` | 95.9 GB | Birthplace splits, cleaned names, 448 matched shards, four consolidated matched files. Renamed from `output_sam/`, which was named after a person and mixed inputs, logs, intermediates and outputs under a name suggesting it held only outputs. |
101:| `temp.dta` | 146 MB | BUNMD male denominator: 20,886,324 men, birth year ≤ 1940. Written by `04_post_match.do`, read by `05_make_linkage_table.do`. **Was written by a bare `save temp`** with no directory, so it landed wherever Stata happened to be started from; nothing in the pipeline `cd`s to `$D_MORT`, so writer and reader only agreed by accident. Now written explicitly to `$D_MORT/temp.dta`. |
102:
103:---
104:
105:## 3. Necessary files per table
106:
107:Each table needs only a handful of files. Expensive matching output is kept on
108:disk deliberately, so tables rebuild in minutes-to-an-hour rather than the many
109:hours a re-match would cost.
110:
