132:
133:---
134:
135:## 4. How each table is built, and what the numbers mean
136:
137:### Shared vocabulary (linkages 1 and 2)
138:
139:Every proposed pair is compared against the ground-truth links and lands in one
140:of three buckets:
141:
142:| Term | Meaning |
143:|---|---|
144:| **Verified correct** | The pair matches a ground-truth link. |
145:| **Directly contradicted** | The 1940 record *is* in the truth set, but linked to a different 1930 person. An outright error. |
146:| **Unverifiable** | The 1940 record does not appear in the truth set. Neither confirmed nor refuted — the truth set does not cover everyone. |
147:| **Match rate** | Linked pairs ÷ eligible population. |
148:| **Recall** | verified ÷ (verified + ground-truth pairs never proposed). |
149:| **MI match rate** | Among links with a middle initial observed on both sides, the share that agree. Middle initials are **not** used for matching, so this is an out-of-sample quality check. |
150:
151:The **restricted** variants repeat everything on the subset where
152:`congruent == 0` — 1940 records whose Ancestry and FamilySearch transcriptions
153:disagree. That is where transcription quality should matter most, and it is where
154:the largest improvements appear.
155:
156:### Table 2 detail — HLINK link cleanup
157:
158:`build_matched_links.do` combines two rounds of HLINK output under one rule:
159:**step 2 supersedes step 1.** A step-1 pair survives only if *neither* of its
160:records appears anywhere in step 2, which catches both collision types — one 1930
161:record linked to two different 1940 records, and two 1930 records linked to the
162:same 1940 record.
163:
164:### Table 3 detail — Mortality
165:
166:| Term | Meaning |
167:|---|---|
168:| **Exact match** | ABE match on cleaned first name, cleaned last name, and harmonized birthplace, unique within a ±2-year birth-year window in both files. |
169:| **Conservative match** | Additionally drops records having *any* competing candidate within ±2 years of birth. |
170:| **Linkage rate** | Matches ÷ 20,886,324 BUNMD men with birth year ≤ 1940. |
171:| **Validation sample** | Links with a middle initial observed in both sources. |
172:
173:---
174:
175:## 5. Results
176:
177:Rebuilt via `code/00_run_all_tables.do`; output in `output/tables/`.
178:
179:### Reproducibility check, 2026-09-06
180:
181:The full master was run end to end after the data reorganisation — a ~110 GB
182:move of the ABE/JW tree into the package, a restructure of `data/` by role, and
183:roughly 60 path changes. **All five tables came back byte-identical** to the
184:pre-reorganisation copies, with one exception that is not a number: the mortality
185:table's first line is a `%` comment naming the file, and the older copy still
186:carried the name it had before the table outputs were renamed. Every figure in
187:every table is unchanged.
188:
189:This is the check that matters after a move of that size. Paths resolving
190:(`code/shared/verify_paths.do`) only proves files were found; reproducing the
191:numbers proves the *right* files were found.
192:
193:Measured runtime, StataMP-64 15.1, ~3 cores, 12.6 GB peak:
194:
195:| Stage | Time |
196:|---|---|
197:| 1/3 ABE + Jaro-Winkler | 88 min |
198:| 2/3 HLINK | 159 min |
199:| 3/3 Mortality | minutes |
200:| **total** | **4 h 07 m** |
201:
202:### Mortality (`linkage_table_mortality_bunmd.tex`)
203:
204:| | Old names | New names | Change |
205:|---|---|---|---|
206:| Exact matches | 5,754,061 | 6,165,698 | +7.15% |
207:| Exact linkage rate | 27.55% | 29.52% | +1.97 pp |
208:| Conservative matches | 4,286,765 | 4,621,152 | +7.80% |
209:| Conservative linkage rate | 20.52% | 22.13% | +1.60 pp |
210:| MI agreement (exact) | 82.54% | 84.70% | +2.16 pp |
211:| MI agreement (conservative) | 87.12% | 88.87% | +1.74 pp |
212:
213:The new transcription links **more** records, and the extra links are **more**
214:accurate, not less — middle-initial agreement rises even as volume grows.
215:
216:### HLINK (`linkage_table_hlink.tex`)
217:
218:| | Old | New |
219:|---|---|---|
220:| Match rate | 58.08% | 59.55% |
221:| Recall | 78.48% | 79.88% |
222:| MI match rate | 86.11% | 86.34% |
223:| Verified correct | 28,357,041 | 28,863,711 |
224:| Directly contradicted | 293,078 | 296,657 |
225:
226:Denominator 115,030,746 (1940 population aged 8+, men and women). Pair changes:
227:4,090,989 added, 2,400,121 removed, 309,900 changed, 64,097,518 unchanged. Of
228:links added, 27.7% verify correct against 1.6% contradicted; of links removed,
229:26.4% were correct against 2.4% contradicted — a favourable trade.
230:
231:Restricted sample (`congruent == 0`, 35,550,163 people): 49.70% → 56.55%, a much
232:larger gain, as expected where the two transcriptions disagree.
233:
234:### ABE / Jaro-Winkler (`linkage_table_abe_jw.tex`)
235:
236:See `output/tables/`.
237:
238:| | JW old | JW new | ABE old | ABE new |
239:|---|---|---|---|---|
240:| Match rate | 22.00% | 23.40% | 21.73% | 23.42% |
241:| MI match rate | 86.25% | 87.00% | 86.27% | 87.00% |
242:| Verified correct | 6,222,698 | 6,627,332 | 6,188,445 | 6,690,920 |
243:| Directly contradicted | 190,805 | 171,174 | 210,054 | 194,387 |
244:| Recall | 31.95% | 34.03% | 31.78% | 34.36% |
245:
246:Both methods improve under the new transcription, and — as with the other two
247:linkages — the added links are disproportionately good: of links added, 42.3% (JW)
248:and 45.1% (ABE) verify correct against roughly 2% contradicted.
249:
250:**Verified against the previous run.** This table reproduces the 2026-04-02
251:version (preserved in `_archive/output_previous/`) **exactly, in every cell**, despite the
252:script having been repointed from dead `D:` and `E:` paths onto the reorganized
253:tree. That makes it a complete regression test of the path repairs.
254:
255:One caution for anyone repeating this check: the ground-truth file
256:`mlp_training1930_1940.dta` carries an mtime of 2026-08-18, four months *after*
257:that table was written, which suggests the truth set changed. It did not — the
258:timestamp records the file being **moved** during reorganization, not edited. An
259:mtime cannot distinguish a move from a content change; the reproduction of the
260:truth-dependent columns is what actually establishes the content is unchanged.
261:
262:---
263:
264:## 6. Known gaps and caveats
265:
266:- **`build_matched_links.do` is a reconstruction.** The original was deleted. Its
267:  body was recovered verbatim from the command echo in
268:  `_archive/output_previous/build_matched_links.log`; its SETTINGS block was *not* in that
269:  echo and has been inferred from paths and counts in the run output. The file
270:  documents exactly which five values are inferred. Its logic was validated
271:  against the independent `prep_HLINK_matches.do` implementation: both produce the
272:  same pair set, matching to the row (66,807,539 / 68,498,407).
273:- **The ABE matcher is a reconstruction, but a validated one.** No script on any
274:  drive referenced `noraceblock`, the string in the ABE link filenames, so the
275:  code that produced `link1930_1940_*_noraceblock0.dta` is gone.
276:  `code/01_abe_jw/linkage/run_abe_match.do` reconstructs it; its header lists
277:  every inferred parameter and the basis for each.
278:  `linkage/validate_abe_reconstruction.do` then tests it against the originals
279:  by **pair**, not by count — counts are weak evidence, since different
280:  parameterisations can land on similar totals. On Massachusetts it reproduced
281:  the originals exactly: the identical 488,439-pair set, zero pairs on either
282:  side alone, and `f_name_A`, `l_name_A`, `f_name_B`, `l_name_B`, `fiveyr_band`,
283:  `mi1930`, `mi1940` all identical. One birthplace exercising every parameter on
284:  1.6M × 1.8M records is strong evidence for the parameter set; it is not proof
285:  that the other 135 reproduce. Whether `f_name_A` holds cleaned or raw names
286:  remains undetermined — the two are identical on every bpl-25 row, so the test
287:  could not discriminate, and output is the same either way.
288:- **The reconstruction has now also been run on the full count** (6.9 h, both
289:  vintages, zero birthplace failures). It reproduces 16,944,747 of 16,946,362
290:  pairs (old) and 18,197,078 of 18,198,099 (new) — 99.99%, and a **strict
291:  subset** both times, producing zero pairs the originals lack. **Every missing
292:  pair is a one-name join**: the 1930 record has a blank first or last name, and
293:  `run_full_census_prep.do` excludes it because a record with no initial cannot
294:  be blocked. In the originals those pairs are blank on the same field on both
295:  sides (`george <blank>` ↔ `george <blank>`), which is the only way an exact
296:  name matcher could have joined them. Diagnosed by
297:  `code/01_abe_jw/linkage/diagnose_abe_shortfall*.do`; see `LINKAGE_README.md`.
298:- **The original ABE links contain a small number of one-name links.** 845 (old)
299:  and 630 (new) of the pairs above carry `fiveyr_band == 1`, so they enter
300:  `linkage_table_abe_jw.tex`. Against 12.5M and 13.5M `fiveyr_band` pairs that is
301:  0.007% — far too small to move any published figure, and it appears on both
302:  sides of the old-vs-new comparison. Recorded because it is a property of the
303:  link files a reader should know, not because it changes a result.
304:- **HLINK matching is third-party.** The step CSVs in `data/links/raw_hlink/` were produced
305:  elsewhere. Only the link cleanup and the table analysis are ours.
306:- **`usa_00015.dat` is missing.** The IPUMS extract behind `marriage_status_1940.dta`
307:  is not on disk. Its *output* is, so no table is blocked. A file with the same
308:  extract number exists at `F:\Deaglan\Indicators\ipums_data\usa_00015.dta` — an
309:  unverified lead, not a confirmed match.
310:- **One live dependency outside the package:**
311:  `D:\ABE_JW\DATA\ORIGINAL\matching_intgen1940.dta`, referenced by
312:  `000_merge_new_census_vars.do`. Provenance only — its output is in `data/census/`.
313:- **`code/03_mortality/README_original.md` is superseded.** It documents pipeline
314:  steps 0-4 and predates `05_make_linkage_table.do`, so the table it produces
315:  appears nowhere in it, and its path examples are stale. Kept as a historical
316:  record of the mortality pipeline's design; this file is authoritative.
317:- **`linkage_table_newlinks*.tex` (in `_archive/`) is not comparable to
318:  `linkage_table_hlink*.tex`.** It came from a retired script with a settings block
319:  (`dedup_rule "prob"`, `unique1940`) that the current script dropped in favour of
320:  `duplicates drop histid1930, force`. A methodological difference, not a
321:  relabelling.
322:- **Transcription shard naming — partially resolved.** `data/transcriptions/`
323:  holds `divided_file{1930,1940}_{ans,true}_cleaned_women_added`, while the ABE/JW
324:  working tree at `$ABEJW/data/full_census/names/` holds
325:  `divided_file{1930,1940}_{ans,true}_cleaned` — the same four names *without* the
326:  `_women_added` suffix. So these are two genuinely distinct vintages, not a
327:  naming inconsistency: the ABE/JW pipeline filters to males, so its shards are
328:  the men-only originals, and the `_women_added` set is the later expansion used
329:  by the HLINK table. `code/03_mortality/0_merge_new_names.do` reads the
330:  unsuffixed names, i.e. the ABE/JW vintage. Still unaccounted for: that script
331:  also wants `divided_file1940_true_cleaned_third_batch`, which exists in neither
332:  location. Off every table path either way.
333:- **`vendor/censocdev-master/` declares no license.** It is an unmodified GitHub
334:  ZIP of the UC Berkeley CenSoc `censocdev` R package (Breen, Alexander, Joo,
335:  Goldstein, Yildirim, Osborne). The mortality `.do` files are a Stata port of its
336:  `codebase/02_create_censoc/` scripts. Resolve licensing before redistributing.
337:- **Denominator naming.** The HLINK script's `N_men_1940_full` scalar now holds a
338:  whole-population count, not a men-only one, because the linked files include
339:  women. The value is correct; the name is stale, as is the "all men in the 1940
340:  census" phrasing in the table captions.
