1:/*------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2:ABE matching (exact on cleaned names, race NOT used as a blocking variable)
3:
4:RECONSTRUCTED. The script that produced
5:    data/links/link1930_1940_ans_cleaned_noraceblock0.dta   (16,946,362 rows)
6:    data/links/link1930_1940_true_cleaned_noraceblock0.dta  (18,198,099 rows)
7:does not exist on any drive here. This is a reconstruction whose parameters were
8:INFERRED from the surviving artifacts. Read the inference table before trusting
9:any output.
10:
11:VALIDATED against the originals on Massachusetts (bpl 25), 2026-09-06:
12:
13:    original links, bpl 25 only :  488,439
14:    reconstruction pairs        :  488,439
15:    in BOTH                     :  488,439   (100.0% both ways)
16:    reconstruction only         :        0
17:    original only               :        0
18:    f_name_A l_name_A f_name_B l_name_B fiveyr_band mi1930 mi1940 : all identical
19:
20:Not merely equal totals -- the identical PAIR SET with identical field values.
21:Reproduce with linkage/validate_abe_reconstruction.do.
22:
23:FULL COUNT, both vintages, 2026-09-06/07 (6.9 h, zero birthplace failures):
24:
25:                        reconstruction      original     difference
26:    ans_cleaned (old)       16,944,747    16,946,362         -1,615
27:    true_cleaned (new)      18,197,078    18,198,099         -1,021
28:
29:99.99% agreement, and a STRICT SUBSET both times: the reconstruction produces
30:ZERO pairs the original lacks. The matching rule is therefore right; the
31:difference is upstream, in which 1930 records reach the matcher.
32:
33:Every one of those missing pairs -- 100% of them, both vintages -- is a ONE-NAME
34:JOIN. Diagnosed in three steps by linkage/diagnose_abe_shortfall*.do:
35:
36:  1. strict subset, and every missing pair's A-side id is absent from the
37:     by_bpl inputs entirely;
38:  2. all of those ids ARE in clean_names_1930_old and are male with bpl and
39:     race, but have a blank first name (791/563) or blank last name (947/593).
40:     run_full_census_prep.do drops them: a record with no initial cannot be
41:     blocked;
42:  3. in the original link file those pairs are blank on the SAME field on BOTH
43:     sides -- "george <blank>" to "george <blank>", "<blank> smith" to
44:     "<blank> smith". ABE matches names exactly, so a blank could only ever
45:     have matched another blank.
46:
47:So the reconstruction is not losing real links; it declines to make links that
48:rest on a single name field. Note that 845 (old) and 630 (new) of them carry
49:fiveyr_band == 1, meaning the ORIGINAL link files -- and the published table
50:built from them -- contain that many one-name links. Against 12.5M and 13.5M
51:fiveyr_band pairs that is 0.007%, far too small to move any published number,
52:but it is a property of the originals worth knowing.
53:
54:Do NOT "fix" this by loosening the matcher. The gap is a deliberate prep-stage
55:exclusion, and reproducing it would mean re-introducing blank-name blocking.
56:
57:bpl is a blocking variable, so the exact Massachusetts result was already strong
58:evidence for the parameter set; the full count now confirms it at scale.
59:
60:One question remains open: whether f_name_A/f_name_B carry CLEANED or RAW names.
61:Both are str16, and on bpl 25 f_name_cleaned and raw f_name are identical on all
62:488,439 rows, so the test could not discriminate. It does not affect output here
63:-- both choices give the same answer -- but a bpl where cleaning actually bites
64:would settle it.
65:
66:--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
67:PARAMETER                 VALUE            BASIS
68:--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
69:match variables           f_name_cleaned   The target files are named
70:                          l_name_cleaned   "*_cleaned_noraceblock*": matching is
71:                                           on the cleaned name fields, and race
72:                                           is NOT a blocking variable even though
73:                                           it is present in the input.
74:                                           bpl is omitted because the inputs are
75:                                           already split one file per bpl, so it
76:                                           is constant within a match.
77:
78:timevar / timediff        age / 10         Census years 1930 -> 1940. Same as
79:                                           comparison/01_abe_exact_matching.do:101.
80:
81:timeband                  2                abematch default. Iterates over age
82:                                           bands +/-0, +/-1, +/-2.
83:
84:unique_m / unique_f       2 / 2            Same as
85:                                           comparison/01_abe_exact_matching.do:129.
86:                                           Creates unique_match1-2, unique_file1-2.
87:
88:fiveyr_band               unique_match2    INFERRED. +/-2 years spans a FIVE-YEAR
89:                          & unique_file2   window (y-2..y+2), so "fiveyr_band"
90:                                           reads as the +/-2 uniqueness flag. This
91:                                           is also exactly the "conservative"
92:                                           criterion used by the mortality
93:                                           pipeline (03_match.do:65-66) and by
94:                                           comparison/01_abe_exact_matching.do:
95:                                           139-140. linkage_analysis.do filters
96:                                           ABE links on fiveyr_band == 1.
97:
98:id_A / id_B               id               CORRECTED after a test run. abematch
99:                                           suffixes the SOURCE VARIABLE NAME, so
100:                                           id_A(histid) emits histid_A, not id_A.
101:                                           The target schema has id_A/id_B, so
102:                                           the original must have renamed
103:                                           histid -> id before matching. The
104:                                           by_bpl inputs carry histid; this
105:                                           script renames it in the prep step.
106:
107:keep_A / keep_B           f_name l_name    Target schema has f_name_A, l_name_A,
108:                                           f_name_B, l_name_B -- i.e. keep_A() on
109:                                           variables named f_name / l_name.
110:
111:mi1930 / mi1940           merged after     Not produced by abematch. Merged back
112:                                           from the clean_names files by histid.
113:                                           CONFIRMED by string width: mi is
114:                                           str13 in clean_names_1930_old and
115:                                           str14 in clean_names_1940_old, which
116:                                           are exactly the widths of mi1930 and
117:                                           mi1940 in BOTH target link files.
118:                                           That pins the source files and the
119:                                           old/new vintage mapping.
120:
121:final variable list       9 vars           The target files carry ONLY the 9
122:                                           schema variables; abematch's working
123:                                           columns (unique_*, timediff_*, age,
124:                                           the match variables, bpl) are dropped.
125:                                           Kept in the per-bpl intermediates for
126:                                           diagnostics, dropped at the final save.
127:--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
128:
129:NYSIIS: NOT USED, and this is deliberate, not an inference.
130:
131:Classic ABE applies NYSIIS phonetic encoding to absorb spelling variation. This
132:project does not, because the names arriving here have ALREADY been cleaned by
133:our own transcription pipeline -- that cleaning is the very thing the three
134:linkages exist to evaluate. Running NYSIIS on top would collapse exactly the
135:distinctions the analysis is measuring, and would make old-vs-new transcription
136:differences partly invisible. The sibling script
137:comparison/01_abe_exact_matching.do disables it for the same reason (nonysiis),
138:and the target filenames say "_cleaned_", not "_nysiis_".
139:
140:The `use_nysiis' setting below is retained at 0 for sensitivity checks only.
141:Setting it to 1 does not reproduce these links and changes match counts
142:materially. Do not flip it without a specific reason.
143:
144:================================================================================
145:RUNNING THIS ON THE FULL COUNT
146:================================================================================
147:
148:Set  vintage "both"  and  bpl_subset ""  and let it run. Expect roughly
149:2.7 hours per vintage, 5.5 hours for both, on one core.
150:
151:That figure is MEASURED, not extrapolated. bpl 1 (Alabama, 3,148,159 records,
152:2.5% of the universe) took 243 s of match time, and the runner's own
153:records-weighted ETA settled at 2.68 h per vintage over the first two
154:birthplaces. Add ~4 min per vintage for the middle-initial merge, which is a
155:fixed cost: it reads two 5-6 GB name files whatever the run size.
156:
157:An earlier estimate of 4-5 h per vintage was too pessimistic. It came from
158:Massachusetts' 5.4 min WALL time, which included Stata startup and file
159:loading; the per-bpl timer above measures matching alone.
160:
161:The estimate is also insensitive to how matching cost scales with block size,
162:because the birthplace distribution is flat: the largest bpl is only 2.89x
163:Massachusetts and the top five hold 29% of all 128,090,207 records. So a bad
164:guess about the scaling exponent moves the answer by well under an hour.
165:
166:Three things make a long run survivable, and all are on by default:
167:
168:  overwrite 0     Per-bpl output already on disk is skipped, so an interrupted
169:                  run resumes where it stopped instead of restarting.
170:  stop_on_error 0 One birthplace failing is recorded and the run continues;
171:                  every failure is listed at the end and the combined file is
172:                  NOT written from a partial set unless allow_partial is set.
173:  progress        Per-bpl timing plus an ETA weighted by RECORDS remaining,
174:                  not birthplaces remaining -- birthplace count is a poor
175:                  progress measure when the largest is 2 million times the
176:                  smallest.
177:
178:This runner is SERIAL. The JW runner beside it uses 12 parallel clusters, and
179:birthplaces are independent by construction, so the same treatment would cut
180:this to well under an hour per vintage. That is deliberately not done here: the
181:reconstruction was validated serially, and adding concurrency would put
182:unvalidated machinery between the inputs and a result we can currently prove is
183:exact. Parallelise it once a full serial run has been checked against the
184:originals.
185:
186:Verify any full run against the known totals above before using the output.
187:------------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
188:
189:drop _all
190:clear all
191:set more off
192:
193:* ---- Path bootstrap: single source of truth ----
194:if "$ROOT" == "" global ROOT "F:/Deaglan/Census_Transcriptions"
195:do "$ROOT/code/shared/paths.do"
196:
197:*==============================================================================
198:* SETTINGS
199:*==============================================================================
200:
201:* Which transcription. "old" reads data/by_bpl, "new" reads
202:* data/by_bpl_new_transcription, "both" runs each in turn.
203:local vintage "both"
204:
205:* Birthplaces to process. "" (empty) = every bpl in bpl_list.dta.
206:* Otherwise a space-separated list, e.g. "25" for Massachusetts alone or
207:* "518 463 538" for a fast end-to-end smoke test on tiny birthplaces.
208:local bpl_subset ""
209:
210:* 0 = skip birthplaces whose output already exists (resumable).
211:* 1 = redo everything.
212:local overwrite 0
213:
214:* 0 = record a failing birthplace and carry on; 1 = abort the whole run.
215:local stop_on_error 0
216:
217:* Write the combined file even if some birthplaces failed or are absent.
218:* Leave at 0: a silently partial link file is worse than no link file.
219:local allow_partial 0
220:
221:* 1 = merge middle initials back on after matching.
222:local add_mi 1
223:
224:* NYSIIS: leave at 0. Names are already cleaned upstream by our own pipeline,
225:* and that cleaning is what these linkages exist to evaluate. See header.
226:local use_nysiis 0
227:
228:*==============================================================================
229:* SET UP
230:*==============================================================================
231:
232:* abematch / abeclean ship with this package
233:adopath + "$C_ABEJW/reference"
234:adopath + "$C_ABEJW/ado"
235:
236:capture which abematch
237:if _rc {
238:    di as error "abematch not found on the adopath."
239:    di as error "  expected at: $C_ABEJW/reference/abematch.ado"
240:    exit 111
241:}
242:
243:if !inlist("`vintage'", "old", "new", "both") {
244:    di as error "vintage must be old, new or both -- got: `vintage'"
245:    exit 198
246:}
247:if "`vintage'" == "both" local vintages "old new"
248:else                     local vintages "`vintage'"
249:
250:*==============================================================================
251:* PER-BPL WORKER
252:*==============================================================================
253:* A program, not inline code, so the whole per-birthplace unit can be wrapped
254:* in -capture- and a single failure cannot take down a multi-hour run.
255:* Results come back in globals because locals do not survive the call.
256:
257:capture program drop abe_one_bpl
258:program define abe_one_bpl
259:    syntax , Bpl(integer) SRCdir(string) OUTfile(string) [NYSiis(integer 0)]
260:
261:    foreach yr in 1930 1940 {
262:        capture confirm file "`srcdir'/clean_names_`yr'.dta"
263:        if _rc {
264:            di as error "  missing input: `srcdir'/clean_names_`yr'.dta"
265:            exit 601
266:        }
267:    }
268:
269:    * ---- prepare both sides ----
270:    * abematch requires the match variables to be present and to compare
271:    * exactly. keep_A()/keep_B() need them named f_name / l_name so the output
272:    * lands as f_name_A / f_name_B, matching the target schema.
273:    tempfile ready30 ready40
274:    foreach yr in 1930 1940 {
275:        use "`srcdir'/clean_names_`yr'.dta", clear
276:        quietly count
277:        global ABE_N`yr' = r(N)
278:
279:        * Match on f_name_cleaned / l_name_cleaned, but ALSO carry copies named
280:        * f_name / l_name so keep_A()/keep_B() emit f_name_A, f_name_B, ...
281:        * exactly as the target schema has them.
282:        quietly generate f_name = f_name_cleaned
283:        quietly generate l_name = l_name_cleaned
284:
285:        * abematch names its output <idvar>_A / <idvar>_B -- it suffixes the
286:        * SOURCE variable name. The target schema has id_A/id_B, so the id
287:        * variable must literally be named id at match time. Rename exactly
288:        * (posof), not -confirm variable-, which accepts abbreviations and
289:        * would match some other id-prefixed variable.
290:        quietly ds
291:        local have `r(varlist)'
292:        local p : list posof "id" in have
293:        if `p' == 0 {
294:            local q : list posof "histid" in have
295:            if `q' == 0 {
296:                di as error "  no id variable in `srcdir'/clean_names_`yr'.dta"
297:                di as error "  looked for: id, histid"
298:                exit 111
299:            }
300:            rename histid id
301:        }
302:
303:        if `nysiis' {
304:            capture confirm variable sex
305:            if _rc quietly generate byte sex = 1
306:            abeclean f_name_cleaned l_name_cleaned, sex(sex)
307:        }
308:
309:        if `yr' == 1930 quietly save `ready30', replace
310:        else            quietly save `ready40', replace
311:    }
312:
313:    * ---- match ----
314:    clear
315:    abematch f_name_cleaned l_name_cleaned,        ///
316:        file_A("`ready30'") file_B("`ready40'")    ///
317:        timevar(age) timediff(10) timeband(2)      ///
318:        id_A(id) id_B(id)                          ///
319:        unique_m(2) unique_f(2)                    ///
320:        keep_A(f_name l_name) keep_B(f_name l_name)
321:
322:    quietly count
323:    global ABE_NRAW = r(N)
324:
325:    * ---- fiveyr_band: unique within the +/-2 (five-year) window ----
326:    * float, not byte: the target files store fiveyr_band as float.
327:    quietly generate float fiveyr_band = (unique_match2 == 1 & unique_file2 == 1)
328:    quietly count if fiveyr_band == 1
329:    global ABE_NBAND = r(N)
330:
331:    quietly generate int bpl = `bpl'
332:    quietly save "`outfile'", replace
